\par\par \b \uc2  Remarks Show/China/Yuan -2: System's Ruthless Trade-Off \b0\par\par\uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 1998\par 01:42\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Under Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -board system, local interest rates rise automatically if there is a speculative attack on the Hong Kong dollar. The system is practically impregnable but it exacts a ruthless trade-off. The high interest rates quickly hurt the rest of the economy, hammering property prices, cutting corporate profits, and leading to worker layoffs. \par \par \uc2  "If you want the peg to remain, higher interest rates are the only way even if the economy (must) bleed," said Wu of DBS. The pain caused by rising rates has exacerbated this city's worst slowdown in more than 20 years. Many politicians have been calling for the government to find ways to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  without using high interest rates. \par \par \uc2  In recent weeks, the Hong Kong government has been trying to damp the pain of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -board system by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the money market and the property market. It is this activity that some observers say is a hint that Hong Kong policy makers may be unwilling to endure the pain of the dollar peg indefinitely. "They're undermining the credibility of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board," said Chris Wood, emerging-markets strategist at Banco Santander Investments. \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the government sold U.S. dollars to buy Hong Kong dollars, an action it took last week as well. A spokeswoman for the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to specify the amount of U.S. dollars sold, but said it was far less than was sold Tuesday and last week. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders said the sales have kept interest rates artificially low. They also said that in recent months the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has injected funds into the interbank market to keep short-term interest rates low. \par \par \uc2  Financial Secretary Sir Donald Tsang said late last week the selling of U.S. dollars had helped to keep local interest rates from spiking a result, he said, "that may please a lot of people." \par \par \uc2  Tsang and other government officials have characterized the selling as necessary to fund a seasonal shortfall of revenue from taxes and duties. They argue it isn't a direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A spokeswoman for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the authority wasn't acting to keep short-term interest rates artificially low, and continued to abide by the discipline of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board. \par \par \uc2  That's not the way some market participants see it. Wood of Banco Santander said that in addition to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the interbank market, the government has also been trying to ease the pain by propping up property prices. In June, the government froze new land sales to stabilize falling property prices. Much of Hong Kong's wealth is tied up in the property market, and as Hong Kong's economy has soured, so too has public opinion of the government's handling of the economy. \par \par \uc2  So far, public opinion in Hong Kong is solidly behind keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg. But Wood predicted that will change as the costs of maintaining the peg rapidly mount. Already, unemployment is at 4.2%, the worst level in more than 15 years. \par \par \uc2  Jim Walker, an economist at Credit Lyonnais Securities (Asia) Ltd., predicted that Hong Kong will be forced to endure "the misery of the peg," in which unemployment more than doubles and the economy contracts by 5.8% next year. In his scenario, Hong Kong might not see positive economic growth until 2001. \par \par \uc2  The key, observers said, is whether the government has the political will to adhere to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -board system. "If they stick with (the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board), they can probably pull through as long as the Chinese leaders don't devalue the yuan," said Wu of DBS. \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 12-08-98 \par \par \uc2  2342GMT \par \par \uc2  1125 62544 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8c06t9x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
