\par\par \b \uc2  AWSJ: Remarks Show Pressures On China To Devalue Yuan \b0\par\par\uc2 596 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 1998\par 01:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  From Thursday's Asian Wall Street Journal \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    By Erik Guyot and Ian Johnson\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Government officials from China and Hong Kong are sending mixed messages about their willingness to bear the economic pain of defending their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, Beijing's ambassador to India said in a speech that further declines in the Japanese yen would hurt China's economy and might force a rethink of China's policy not to devalue its yuan. His comments appeared to contradict statements a day earlier by a top official from the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , who reiterated China's commitment to maintaining its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's level. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  the government continued on Wednesday to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in local money markets to cushion the pain of higher interest rates caused by its exchange-rate system. That activity is leading some to question the political will of Hong Kong's government to maintain its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -board system at all costs. \par \par \uc2  Wednesday's events underscore the extent to which China and Hong Kong are facing rising domestic pressures as their strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  hurt certain sectors of their economies. The yuan and the Hong Kong dollar are the last major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia left standing with exchange rates fixed to the U.S. dollar, in the wake of Asia's yearlong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis. \par \par \uc2  At a speech in New Delhi, China's Ambassador Zhou Gang said that "in my view a further depreciation of the yen would force China to have a second thinking" on whether to devalue. Economists cautioned that Zhou's comments probably didn't reflect Beijing's current thinking, but instead reiterated a previous government policy in which Chinese leaders made oblique threats to devalue the yuan if the yen continued to tumble. \par \par \uc2  Nevertheless, Zhou's remarks highlight the pressures on Beijing to devalue the yuan. A growing number of companies engaged in heavy industry located in China's coastal provinces have been clamoring for a devaluation, which would help them compete with cheaper products from South Korea, Taiwan and elsewhere in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Beijing isn't expected to devalue any time soon, since that wouldn't help China's economy many companies must also import large quantities of raw materials and foreign machinery. The yuan's decline would not only raise the cost of imports and overseas borrowings but also most likely plunge the rest of Asia into another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciations. \par \par \uc2  A devaluation of the yuan would also undermine confidence in the Hong Kong government's ability to keep the Hong Kong dollar trading near its fixed rate of HK$7.8 to the U.S. dollar. Although Beijing and Hong Kong operate their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  systems independently, in the minds of many investors, the two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are intertwined. \par \par \uc2  So interconnected are the two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , that in recent weeks some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators have been attacking the Hong Kong dollar as a way of making a proxy bet that the yuan eventually will decline in value. China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't freely traded, so \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators take bets that the Hong Kong dollar will tumble if the yuan depreciates. \par \par \uc2  In trying to ward off those speculative attacks, the Hong Kong government has been sending mixed messages as to its political will to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  whatever the cost, some observers said. "They are sending a confusing signal," said Fredrich Wu, chief economist at DBS Bank in Singapore. "That's why the Hong Kong dollar is under attack." \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 12-08-98 \par \par \uc2  2322GMT \par \par \uc2  1125 62544 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8c06t8w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
