\par\par \b \uc2  AWSJ: HK Hit On Fears Of Too-Expensive Property Prices \b0\par\par\uc2 447 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 1998\par 01:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    From Wednesday's Asian Wall Street Journal\par    By Erik Guyot\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Concerns about the Hong Kong dollar's future are roiling financial markets in the former British colony, as a new worry gets factored in: Property prices - which have already slid 40% from their 1997 peak - might not be falling nearly quickly enough. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, local interest rates soared: Hong Kong dollar overnight interest rates jumped to 8%, up from 6.5% on Monday, as speculators continued to test the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength. Meanwhile, the benchmark Hang Seng Index tumbled 3.6% to its lowest level in five years. Since the beginning of August, the index has fallen 15%. \par \par \uc2  Through it all, the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rigid link to the U.S. dollar remained largely unbudged. The Hong Kong dollar traded in a close range around 7.75 to the U.S. dollar Tuesday, near its official rate of 7.80. \par \par \uc2  For the second time in two weeks, however, the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it sold U.S. dollars, although a spokeswoman declined to say how many. She declined to characterize the sale as an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, saying the measure aimed to raise Hong Kong dollars to help fund a temporary deficit in the government's budget. \par \par \uc2  Weighing on Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been fears that China might need to devalue the yuan to boost its sagging export growth. In public comments on Tuesday, an official at China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  vigorously denied that it would devalue. Many investors say a fall in the value of the yuan would make the Hong Kong dollar's defense far more difficult. \par \par \uc2  But the Hong Kong dollar is feeling pressure as well from a problem closer to home: its troubled property market. Hong Kong property prices soared to astronomical values in recent years, only to fall sharply amid Asia's economic slowdown. But many observers say the 40% decline in the past year isn't enough to make Hong Kong competitive. And as long as Hong Kong isn't competitive economically, its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will feel the heat. \par \par \uc2  "The pressure on the Hong Kong dollar will continue to mount unless the property market can adjust quickly," says Frank Gong, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis has made many Asian cities much more inexpensive places to do business, because of falling property prices and wilting local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Meanwhile, the Hong Kong dollar stands alone as the last freely traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia that has held its value. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 11-08-98 \par \par \uc2  2315GMT \par \par \uc2  1121 62544 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8b06ssq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
