\par\par \b \uc2  HKMA's  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  defence dilemma raises questions. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Mishi Saran \par \uc2 622 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 1998\par 10:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Aug 8 (Reuters) - Will Hong Kong be able to match last week's nimble defence of the local dollar the next time \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators flex their muscles amid Asia's economic gloom? economists asked on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  "The first time speculators attacked Hong Kong, (in October 1997) the government hiked interest rates, this time they used the buying technique, what will they do next?", said Ricky Tam, Senior Research Manager, at Delta Asia Financial Group. "I think the government and the HKMA are improving their techniques." \par \par \uc2  "I am quite impressed with the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA)," Tam added. \par \par \uc2  The territory's de-facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in quickly during last week's massive selling of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by buying undisclosed amounts of Hong Kong dollars on behalf of the Treasury. The HKMA said it needed to fill a shortfall in government coffers. \par \par \uc2  "The mechanism is similar to an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said George Chan, research manager at Celestial Asia Securities Holdings. \par \par \uc2  Since Wednesday, four U.S. investment banks as well as fund houses have been selling the Hong Kong dollar short, with several billion U.S. dollars worth of Hong Kong dollars changing hands in both spot and forward markets, dealers said on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The government action - not, technically an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - helped balance the supply and demand of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars in the market and staved off the threat of a hike in interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  usually relies on a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system, which maintains the Hong Kong dollar's link to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  "It was on an auto pilot system, allowing interest rates to govern the supply and demand of the Hong Kong dollar and make the adjustments," said Paul Tang, senior economist at the Bank oF East Asia. \par \par \uc2  The trouble is, higher interest rates shackle an already beleaguered economy and impoverished Hong Kong can hardly afford another rise in interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Interbank rates had risen on market jitters, but bank deposit rates had not surged, as in the past. "It shows the impact of speculation on bank deposit rates so far is not as severe...it may show that the HKMA has found a way to fend off speculators," Tang said. \par \par \uc2  On Friday the overnight rate hovered around eight percent, just a few percentage points over the five to six percent the overnight rate has averaged over the past few months. In comparison, last October's overnight rate soared to 300 percent. Financial Secretary Donald Tsang hastened to clarify on Friday that the government was not tossing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system. \par \par \uc2  "I am not departing from the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system at all. It so happened that this is a dry month for our Treasury. Usually we go and change some of our \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  into Hong Kong dollars anyway to meet our payments, which occurs this time of the year," he said. \par \par \uc2  Economists cautiously welcomed the move, but did not rule out future hikes in interest rates should the speculators come back. \par \par \uc2  Certainly the stock markets fear a hike in interest rates. On Friday the blue-chip Hang Seng Index fell 3.25 percent to 7,018. Hong Kong's troubles weighed on other Asian markets. \par \par \uc2  And speculators may well return - Financial Secretary Tsang said on Saturday he expected speculators to attack the Hong Kong dollar for as long as Asia remains in crisis. \par \par \uc2  Delta's Tam said that regardless of which method the HKMA used then, the main thing was to have enough reserves of US dollars. Unlike its unfortunate neighbours, on that front Hong Kong need not worry, with US$96.5 billion safely in the bank. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021113du8802rlo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
