\par\par \b \uc2  AWSJ: Stability Of HK Dollar Remains A Worry \b0\par\par\uc2 417 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 August 1998\par 01:02\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  From Friday's Asian Wall Street Journal \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    By Erik Guyot\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Stock prices in Hong Kong plunged to their lowest level in 3 years over concerns about the stability of this city's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Global investors are already actively dumping the shares and bonds of Hong Kong companies with unhedged U.S. dollar debts. "Investors are very concerned" about a possible decline in the value of the Hong Kong dollar, said Raja Visweswaran, head of fixed-income research at Bank of America Asia Ltd. "If companies can prove they have hedged themselves, then automatically they will command a premium" for their securities. \par \par \uc2  The blue-chip Hang Seng Index fell 2.8% Thursday, while local interest rates spiked, triggered by concerns that the continued slide in the Japanese yen might induce China to devalue its yuan, a move that is widely seen as weakening the strength of the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  In trading Thursday, the Hong Kong dollar stayed in a tight range of about 7.75 \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars to the U.S. dollar, thanks to earlier \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Although the government's actions helped bring down overnight interest rates to 5.5%, from 7% late Wednesday, long-term interest rates continued to rise over concerns about the Hong Kong dollar. The key three-month interbank rate rose to 11.13% Thursday from 10.21% on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Regional money markets were also hit by worries that if the Hong Kong dollar were to fall, it could trigger another round of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciations. The Singapore dollar weakened to S$1.74 against the U.S. dollar, from S$1.72 on Wednesday. The Thai baht eased to 41.45 baht to the U.S. dollar, from 40.95 baht at the close of trading Wednesday. The Malaysian ringgit slid to 4.17 ringgit against the dollar, from 4.14 ringgit the previous day. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar is the last freely traded Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that hasn't depreciated, thanks to the government's efforts to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pegged near a rate of 7.8 Hong Kong dollars to the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And although Hong Kong boasts more than US$96 billion in foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves to defend its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the actions by domestic corporations in hedging their U.S.-dollar exposure could weaken the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 06-08-98 \par \par \uc2  2302GMT \par \par \uc2  1121 4279 62541 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8606oni\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
