\par\par \b \uc2  HK's Yam says Q2 unlikely to improve on poor Q1. \b0\par\par\uc2 666 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Juli 1998\par 10:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, July 13 (Reuters) - Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief executive Joseph Yam said on Monday that the territory's second quarter economic performance was unlikely to be an improvement over the first quarter of 1998. \par \par \uc2  The government has estimated that the economy contracted two percent in the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  Yam also said in an interview with Reuters Television that the outlook for the rest of the year could be poor as well. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think the second quarter was an improvement over the first at all and perhaps the third quarter may not be good either," Yam said. \par \par \uc2  "But quite a lot will depend on the external environment and the sentiment is so adverse at this time that we may actually see a further slowdown in the economy before we see a recovery," he said. \par \par \uc2  Yam noted the Hong Kong government was projecting a HK$20 billion fiscal deficit in 1998/99 compared with an HK$80 billion surplus in 1997/98 due to the slowing economy and because of increased spending to soften the pain. \par \par \uc2  "But the public sector of Hong Kong is actually quite small so we can't possibly be spending our way out of a recession. A lot actually depends on the external environment but the external environment is still uncertain and quite unfavorable," he said. \par \par \uc2  Yam said the outlook for Asia would depend largely on how successful Japan was in reflating its economy and sorting out its financial problems. \par \par \uc2  The defeat of Japan's ruling Liberal Democratic Party in upper house election over the weekend and the subsequent resignation of Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto might delay implementation of reforms, but would not derail the process. \par \par \uc2  "Of course, the political situation means these measures may be delayed a little, although I don't think there will be a change in the direction of these measures," he said. \par \par \uc2  Continued volatility in the yen could spur destabilising \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations in the region. \par \par \uc2  "The sentiment is such that continued volatility in the yen could spark another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation in Asia and may undermine the recovery efforts in Asia," said Yam. \par \par \uc2  "But I hope sentiment can actually be changed by greater cooperation among the G7 or G3 countries in helping Japan in coming out of its problems. I think that is in the interest of all of us," he said. \par \par \uc2  Yam, who was in Tokyo for a meeting of the Bank for International Settlements, said he told the group there was a need for greater stability because instability in the yen would have serious implications for the rest of the region and the contagion effects could spread to European and American markets. \par \par \uc2  The HKMA chief said the authority would "absolutely not" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar's peg to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  He said interest rates automatically rose in response to selling pressure on the Hong Kong dollar through the workings of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system. \par \par \uc2  With "a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system you don't need to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. You have some sort of convertible undertaking and if people sell Hong Kong dollars to (the HKMA) we just have to take it. We are entirely passive," he said. \par \par \uc2  Yam said there was no reason for China to devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even if it failed to meet its 1998 growth target of eight percent. \par \par \uc2  China continued to run a balance of trade surplus, had substantial foreign reserves of US$140 billion and maintained capital controls, he said. \par \par \uc2  "The economy is still growing, although it may not be eight percent - I think they may be able to achieve eight percent - even if it is less than eight percent, then so what? It's still quite a significant growth rate and there is no reason for the RMB to be devalued," Yam said. \par \par \uc2  - Andrea Ricci (852) 2843-6371; Fax (852) 2845-0636. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du7d011k0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
