\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Camdessus-yen fall is "crisis within crisis". \b0\par\par\uc2 821 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Juni 1998\par 17:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  STRASBOURG, France, June 23 (Reuters) - The fall in the yen is creating a crisis within the Asian crisis, threatening growth across the region and adding to market instability, the head of the International Monetary Fund said on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "A new crisis - a crisis within the crisis - has emerged with the dramatic fall of the yen," IMF Managing Director Michel Camdessus said in a speech to the parliamentary assembly of the Council of Europe. \par \par \uc2  "This new development reflects a serious lack of confidence, provoked by Japan's sluggish reaction to an economic slowdown that is turning into recession and the crisis in its financial institutions." \par \par \uc2  Camdessus, in a far-reaching speech dealing with the causes of Asia's financial turmoil and what should be done to prevent it happening again, also urged China and \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  not to devalue their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and said \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would only work if Japan corrected its economic weaknesses. \par \par \uc2  "The joint \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the United States and Japan on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market... were justified, but the effect will be lasting only if Japan promptly adopts energetic measures for the rehabilitation of its banking sector, tax relief, public investment and the opening up and deregulation of its economy." \par \par \uc2  Last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the first time in more than six years that the United States had stepped into \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the yen, which had been trading at eight-year lows around 146.75 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  The yen climbed in response to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar sales, although markets are now seeking evidence of concrete action by Japan to ease its financial woes. \par \par \uc2  Camdessus said the yen's fall could slow the recovery of other troubled Asian economies and was already adding to the instability affecting Russia and some emerging economies. \par \par \uc2  Russia, one of the IMF's biggest borrowers before the Asian financial crisis, is now seeking an extra $10 billion or $15 billion to ease its latest financial problems. A team of IMF experts is in Moscow to review the economy and examine the request. \par \par \uc2  Camdessus called on Russia to reform its tax collection system if it wanted money from the international community. \par \par \uc2  "International markets want to see Russia tackle what constitutes its fundamental problem, that is its tax collection system," he told a news conference after the speech. \par \par \uc2  Listing his priorities for the international community, Camdessus stressed the need to hold exchange rate pegs in places like China and Hong Kong and he said the two should be ready to ease fiscal policy if needed. \par \par \uc2  "We must help preserve the stability of exchange rates in countries whose actions may be crucial to market stability, such as China and Hong Kong, despite the negative short-term impact of the crisis on their exports and growth," he said. \par \par \uc2  In these countries, as in Singapore and Taiwan, there is a margin for fiscal stimulus, which should be used if needed, he said. \par \par \uc2  China and its Hong Kong Special Administrative Region have insisted they will not devalue. But exports have been hit by Japan's economic weakness and by problems elsewhere in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Camdessus also called on countries with balance of payments surpluses, including some European states, to do more to help the troubled Asian states, for example with untied loans to countries going through the adjustment process. \par \par \uc2  Rich countries should step up humanitarian aid and improve fiscal management to guard against further economic downturns. \par \par \uc2  Camdessus said last year's IMF reform programs for Thailand, Indonesia and South Korea were designed to restore investor confidence, reestablish access to international capital markets and reverse excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciations. \par \par \uc2  At the news conference, he defended the IMF's recipe for economic recovery and said reforms in South Korea and Thailand, where Fund prescriptions were quickly undertaken, had started to bear fruit. \par \par \uc2  He said Indonesia, whose acceptance of IMF medicine has been far less willing, was only at the beginning of its road to recovery. \par \par \uc2  "One shouldn't think that we are dogmatic in monetary matters and are looking for the highest possible interest rates to produce high \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Camdessus said. \par \par \uc2  The IMF has been accused of rigidly relying on a one-size-fits-all approach to economic crises regardless of how they may differ from country to country. \par \par \uc2  "We told Korea to give itself some budgetary space to produce a greater effect on the economy without having too much impact on the exchange rate. The same thing happened in Thailand and is starting to produce results," he said. \par \par \uc2  "On the other hand, we are at the beginning in Indonesia. We think the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is going to have to be brought back into balance," he said, noting that inflation in Indonesia was expected to reach 80 percent this year. ((Paris newsroom, +33 1 4221 5452, fax +33 1 4236 1072, paris.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0