\par\par \b \uc2  HKMA to keep HK$14 bln in stock market-paper. \b0\par\par\uc2 235 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juni 1998\par 06:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, June 20 (Reuters) - The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) said it would not sell its portfolio of HK$14 billion invested in the local stock market when the Land Fund and Exchange Fund were merged, a local paper said on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  "It is obviously not a good time to sell," said HKMA chief Joseph Yam in a report by the South China Morning Post. \par \par \uc2  The Land Fund, now at HK$195.6 billion, was created in 1983 to hold half of the revenues from government land sales. Money in this fund can be invested in both local and regional stock markets. \par \par \uc2  The Land Fund lost HK$3.8 billion in value in the wake of the economic turmoil that hit the territory and neighbouring countries and has cut its exposure to equities. \par \par \uc2  The government has announced plans to merge the Land Fund and Exchange Fund on April 1 next year. This has created uncertainty over whether the new fund will be able to invest or not, given the potential for a conflict of interest. \par \par \uc2  The HK$636.6 billion Exchange Fund's statutory role is to influence the exchange value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, usually by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the local money or \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to maintain \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability. This fund may not invest in local or regional markets. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6k02c2k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
