\par\par \b \uc2  CORRECTED-FOCUS-Asian leaders applaud yen  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2 712 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  In SINGAPORE story headlined "FOCUS-Asian leaders applaud yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " please read in 13th paragraph....Taiwan's state-run Central News Agency...instead of....China's state-run Central News Agency... (correcting reference). \par \par \uc2  A corrected story follows. \par \par \uc2  By Jason Szep \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, June 18 (Reuters) - Coordinated U.S.-Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the troubled yen won loud applause on Thursday from Asian leaders, lighting a flicker of hope in the otherwise gloomy region. \par \par \uc2  Official reaction was fast and most hoped fresh yen strength would help give Japan better footing to pull Asia out of its economic mess. \par \par \uc2  "We are glad to see that the exchange rate of the Japanese yen is picking up," said China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Dai Xianglong. \par \par \uc2  He was quoted by China's official Xinhua news agency as telling Asian bankers in Beijing that a devaluation of the Chinese yuan was not necessary. \par \par \uc2  Other Asian leaders and central banks joined in the praise for the dollar sales, with many hoping that a firmer yen would help prevent China from abandoning promises not to devalue the yuan, a move that would push Asia's financial crisis to new depths. \par \par \uc2  "I am glad to see that the Japanese yen rebounded yesterday after strong \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief executive Tung Chee-hwa Tung told reporters during a visit to New Zealand. \par \par \uc2  "The key is now for Japan to move forward," he said, adding that the revitalisation of the Japanese economy "will have a great deal of impact on us all". \par \par \uc2  Japanese Prime Minister Ryutaro Hashimoto said he was happy with the impact of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the market. \par \par \uc2  "I am happy with the fact that the joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was appreciated by the market," he told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers estimated the Bank of Japan and the New York Federal Reserve spent up to $6 billion to boost the yen, which rallied to about 136 to the dollar from about 142. The yen remained fairly steady in early Asian trade on Thursday but remained under pressure amid concerns over further \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Stock prices surged on Wall Street and in Asia in reaction to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which also gave regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  a much-needed boost. \par \par \uc2  "The move will help stabilise Asian financial markets," said Taiwan's Finance Minister Paul Chiu. \par \par \uc2  "And after the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates stabilise, it will also be positive to our economy and financial markets," Chiu was quoted late on Wednesday by Taiwan's state-run Central News Agency as saying. \par \par \uc2  Some officials said the move should help take pressure off China, which has seen its exports plunge as the yen crumbled. \par \par \uc2  "It was especially meaningful because the recent depreciation of the yen was increasing pressure on the Chinese yuan, whose depreciation will have a strong shock throughout Asia," said a senior official at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Korea. \par \par \uc2  The head of the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's banking department, Tassana Rachatapoh, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  provided some reprieve to the baht, which jumped in early trading to 40.00-41.00 per dollar from 43.00/43.12 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "This is a move in a right direction. If the yen keeps plunging, the impact would be enormous," Tassana said. \par \par \uc2  "But at the end of the day, Japan will need to sort out the fundamental problems, which include the troubled financial institutions. However, we welcome the move which should help lift the sentiment near-term," she added. \par \par \uc2  New Zealand Prime Minister Jenny Shipley described the concerted support of the yen "helpful". \par \par \uc2  "Japan is such an important trading partner that whatever is happening there will affect not only our trading position, but also our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," she said. \par \par \uc2  "So what has happened overnight is helpful, but I will only personally have real confidence in whether or not we are going to find our way out if we see the sorts of reforms undertaken in Japan that not only I but very many other APEC leaders have discussed on many occasions in the recent past." \par \par \uc2  Jean-Michel Severino, World Bank vice president for East Asia and the Pacific, who said on Tuesday that Asia was plunging into depression, described the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as positive. \par \par \uc2  "It's a very positive step forward," he told reporters in Sydney. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6i02k0e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
