\par\par \b \uc2  Hong Kong interest rates to rise as yen slides. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sarah Davison \par \uc2 664 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Juni 1998\par 02:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, June 16 (Reuters) - Hong Kong interest rates were expected to continue rising on Tuesday after the yen slid through 146 to the U.S. dollar on Monday, casting a nervous pall across Asia. \par \par \uc2  "The yen is the dominant factor at this point," said Andy Xie, economist at Morgan Stanley Dean Witter in \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets were already on edge after heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in New York last weekend by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA). \par \par \uc2  The de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent heavily to defend the Hong Kong dollar after it came under heavy selling pressure. \par \par \uc2  But U.S. dollars used in Hong Kong's defence required the withdrawal of an equivalent amount of local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Hong Kong's money supply, in keeping with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board mechanism linking Hong Kong's dollar to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  That tightened liquidity in the system, and added to pressure on rates. The overnight lending rate was bid at an intra-day high of 16 percent from 10 percent on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Rates also suffered due to a rout in the Hong Kong stock market. Investors fled, pummelling the blue chip Hang Seng index 5.72 percent weaker by the end of the day to 7,462.50. \par \par \uc2  These capital outflows added to the pressure on the Hong Kong dollar, assisted by unconfirmed reports that Chinese Vice Premier Li Lanqing complained about the weak yen and hinted at some shift in the official position on yuan depreciation. \par \par \uc2  Economists and analysts doubted the veracity of the report, which China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it was checking. But markets reacted anyway, confirming the heightened sensitivity in Asia to a possible yuan devaluation. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar remained steadfast despite the gyrations in interest rates, trading in a narrow range around HK$7.7420 after the weekend's successful defence of the key HK$7.7500 level in New York. \par \par \uc2  But there was little relief in sight with markets closing on news that unemployment in Hong Kong hit a 15-year record high of 4.2 percent in March-May, up from 3.9 percent in February-April. \par \par \uc2  "If the situation continues and people are worried about the Hong Kong dollar and there is continued selling of the Hong Kong dollar on the spot, the liquidity situation will get worse," said Frank Gong, senior economist at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  Gong believes the overnight rate could reach 30 percent on Tuesday. Rate direction in Hong Kong has become much clearer following the HKMA's decision to reveal the size of Hong Kong dollar movements in and out of the banking system. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's page on Reuters showed that early on Monday HK$7.2 billion was expected to leave the money supply on Tuesday but that figure fell to HK$1.46 billion by early Tuesday morning. \par \par \uc2  "We have a much better handle on rates between now and the next three days and it clearly indicates rates will be under pressure," said Clive MacDonnell, economist at SG Securities. \par \par \uc2  Liquidity seemed to ease later in the afternoon on Monday, with the overnight rate dropping back to 7.0-12 percent as higher rates attracted deposits. But \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards remained high, confirming market nervousness about future rate direction, and overnight had reached 13-18 percent by Tuesday morning. \par \par \uc2  Three-month forwards were steady at 1,630-1,830 on Tuesday morning, little changed from 1,750-1,950 at Monday's open. \par \par \uc2  "People are viewing that it is prudent to hedge their Hong Kong dollar positions," said MacDonnell. \par \par \uc2  "That may just be prudence rather than speculation, but as long as forwards are higher there will be selling of Hong Kong dollars for U.S. dollars, and that indicates further tightness in the money market." \par \par \uc2  - Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843 6470; Fax (852) 2845 0636 \par \par \uc2  - Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6g02tau\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
