\par\par \b \uc2  HK dlr market eases after weekend's cash squeeze. \b0\par\par\uc2 522 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juni 1998\par 07:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, June 15 (Reuters) - Hong Kong short-term interbank rates spiked higher on Monday in response to a "capital outflow wedge" that started on Friday when the Hong Kong dollar touched 7.75 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Someone has shorted the Hong Kong dollar, either because of capital outflow or to finance speculation," said Andrew Fung, head of capital markets at Commonwealth Bank of Australia. \par \par \uc2  Early on Monday, short-term interbank rates moved higher, with overnight rates quoted at 11.00-14.00 percent compared with 10.00-11.00 percent in late trade on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The three-month rate was quoted at 14.00-18.00 percent on Monday morning compared to 12.00-13.00 percent in late Friday trade. It had eased to 13.00-16.00 in early afternoon trade at 0530 GMT. \par \par \uc2  Tomorrow next-day rates reached as high as 15.00-25.00 percent. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  is believed to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a rate of 7.75 last Friday, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Last Thursday the HKMA began releasing aggregate balance positions information on Reuters page HKMAOOB. \par \par \uc2  That showed on Monday morning that based on spot \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions on Friday, by Tuesday the banking system was expected to return HK$7.2 billion to the HKMA for deposit. \par \par \uc2  "This is a capital outflow wedge," said Fung. "There's not enough HK dollars to deliver to the HKMA." \par \par \uc2  By 0230GMT on Monday, the figure had fallen to HK$3.9 billion, suggesting that banks were more willing to lend and that liquidity in the Hong Kong dollar money market had eased slightly. \par \par \uc2  "That means that there has been some switching back into Hong Kong dollars," said a treasurer at major European bank. \par \par \uc2  "Actually, we have been seeing some corporates placing long term Hong Kong dollars today," he said. \par \par \uc2  In relative terms the amount of money being moved out of Hong Kong dollars and into U.S. dollars has grown since the last major spike in interest rates in October, traders said. \par \par \uc2  At that time, three-month interbank rates touched levels over 40 percent. \par \par \uc2  "There is a bigger amount of money being switched into U.S. dollars from the corporate side," another trader said. "I think the speculators are there, but then there is genuine demand from the corporate side to buy U.S. dollars over the past few weeks." \par \par \uc2  For the very short term, dealers were optimistic interest rates would fall, but not by much. \par \par \uc2  "Already it is down from this morning's high levels," said another European bank trader, "so I believe this may be further reduced as the day or the week goes on." \par \par \uc2  But some traders said if dollar-yen goes up further during the week, that may give rise to further speculators buying US against Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  "There is a very fine balance right now between those who believe in the peg and those who don't," one senior trader said. - Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2843 6345; Fax (852) 2845 0636 - Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6f0332h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
