\par\par \b \uc2  ASIA MARKETS SUMMARY-China, weak yen spark shivers. \b0\par\par\uc2 933 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 1998\par 11:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  0925 GMT - The yen's persistent weakness and renewed fears of a Chinese yuan devaluation shook Asian markets on Wednesday, sending a chill through much of north Asia. \par \par \uc2  Signs the year-old \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis is entering its second stage, with several Asian economies teetering on the brink of recession, fuelled the bearish mood. \par \par \uc2  "It's a vicious circle at the moment and everything is just being melted into the same big Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pot. I can't see any way out," a U.S. bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  YEN STILL UNDER THE GUN \par \par \uc2  The dollar slipped below 141 yen in late Asian trade after U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers said G7 nations had discussed the possible dangers of a weak yen and would continue to monitor \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements. \par \par \uc2  But it remained well supported near the high-140 yen levels, with few traders expecting any major policy announcement from the G7 deputies meeting or any co-ordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "At the moment, it's hard for them to sell dollars aggressively against the yen because the market still wants to buy dollars," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG MARKETS STRICKEN \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong shares dived on worries about the weak yen, with the blue chip Hang Seng Index losing over five percent at one point to hit a new 1998 low of 7,906.39 points. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar was quoted near the key 7.75 level against the U.S. dollar amid rumours the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy it earlier. \par \par \uc2  Forward rates shot up, with three-months at 950/1,100 against 640/690 at Tuesday's close and six-months at 1,950/2,150 against 1,500/1,600. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said funds appeared to be positioning for another speculative attack on the Hong Kong dollar peg amid speculation the territory would be forced to de-link its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the U.S. dollar if China devalued the yuan. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong leader Tung Chee-hwa attempted to calm investor fears, saying his administration was determined to keep the Hong Kong dollar peg. \par \par \uc2  Tung also said \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2 's administration would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market. \par \par \uc2  YUAN FEARS RESURFACE \par \par \uc2  The market was spooked by comments from China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor, who warned on Tuesday that the weak yen was having a severe impact on Beijing's foreign trade. \par \par \uc2  The head of the People's Bank of China, Dai Xianglong, did not explicitly rule out a devaluation of the yuan in response to the tumbling yen, but he simply noted promises China had made in the past not to devalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in reaction to the Asian crisis. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN MARKETS BLUDGEONED \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's dollar skidded through a series of 11-year lows, falling as far as 35.149 to the U.S. dollar on worries about China and the weak yen. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said most of its support came from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it breached the 35 level, but they said the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were not aggressive and most expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to head lower as it caught up with its counterparts in the region. \par \par \uc2  They said a substantial portion of the U.S. dollar demand came from offshore banks in the non-deliverable forwards market. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan stocks were also badly hit and lost over three percent to end at a seven-month low, prompting Finance Minister Paul Chiu to appeal for calm. \par \par \uc2  AUSSIE DOLLAR UNDERMINED, INDIAN RUPEE RATTLED \par \par \uc2  The contagion was far-reaching, with Australia's dollar spiralling towards an all-time low of $0.5710/20 and the Indian rupee trading at fresh lows of 42.40 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Analysts saw little respite before the $0.55 level for the Aussie, which is under fire from the yen's decline, renewed regional instability, weak commodity prices and a market perception Australia simply cannot afford to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through higher interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Indian Commerce Minister Ramakrishna Hegde said the rupee's drop was a cause for concern, but added that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would stabilise in due course. \par \par \uc2  RUPIAH DRIFTS DOWN \par \par \uc2  The rupiah slid to lows of around 12,950 on local demand for dollars, but traders said flows remained thin and commercially driven. \par \par \uc2  President Habibie told Reuters in an interview the stability of the rupiah was more important than its actual level against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  He said a stable rupiah and low inflation were possible "if and only if" growth in the money supply was tempered. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the rupiah could also be undermined by prolonged weakness in the yen, which has already eclipsed the benefits of Indonesia's foreign debt rescheduling and the IMF's plan to resume lending. \par \par \uc2  The market was also likely to keep a close watch on President Habibie's efforts at political reform. Habibie said he had no plans to continue as head of state after the next presidential poll, which he expected around December next year. \par \par \uc2  REST OF SOUTHEAST ASIA STAGGERS \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar, Malaysian ringgit and Thai baht were also hit as their stock markets each lost between three and five percent on the regional jitters. \par \par \uc2  The Sing dollar briefly fell through the 1.73 level against the U.S. dollar as players continued to sell it as a proxy for the region. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht was the wrong side of 43.00 to the dollar, but its fall was somewhat cushioned by commercial demand for the first tranche of 50 billion baht in state-backed domestic bonds, due to be floated this week. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6a03bys\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
