\par\par \b \uc2  ASIAN EXOTICS-Won holds despite jump in inflation. \b0\par\par\uc2 643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  0350 GMT - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mostly easier by mid-morning as dealers waded through a series of comments by regional \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials, but found little to surprise them. \par \par \uc2  * In South Korea, the won held on to most of its gains despite news of soaring inflation, yet more worrying evidence of the impact of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis. \par \par \uc2  The producer price index soared 17.5 percent year-on-year in April, against a 3.8 percent rise the year before. The consumer price index was up 8.8 percent year-on-year in April against a 4.3 percent the year before. \par \par \uc2  * The finance ministry said South Korea's provisional external liabilities at the end of March totalled $151.3 billion against $150.3 billion at the end of February. \par \par \uc2  * The rupiah pared some of its losses but remained below the 8,000 per dollar level, ignoring \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Sjahril Sabirin's comment that interest rates would stay high for the time being to support the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  * Sabirin said Bank Indonesia wanted to see the rupiah exchange rate go below 6,000 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  * Dealers said the forward market was already pricing in the prospect of higher interest rates. \par \par \uc2  "But the spot (dollar/rupiah) rate looks like it's not going above 8,200. It came off very fast from that level yesterday. I think the market is holding long dollar positions so we could see it lower," a U.S. bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  * A senior IMF official said the fund would make its next proposed $3 billion payment to Indonesia in three installments and added that the fund had misjudged the crisis in its early stage. \par \par \uc2  * The Thai baht was weak as the market turned apprehensive about its recent rally amid signs the government might not want it to strengthen further. \par \par \uc2  * Deputy Prime Minister Supachai Panitchpakdi said overnight he was happy with an exchange rate of 40 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  * "I don't see the baht continuing to strengthen from here once the buying euphoria is over. But we could see continuous (dollar) sell offers if there is more investment in Thai companies," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  * In the Philippines, pre-election jitters continued to undermine the peso despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate hikes to stem speculative pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ahead of the May 11 poll. \par \par \uc2  * The central raised its overnight borrowing and lending rates by 50 basis points each, a move that was widely expected after it offered one-day reverse repos at 13.5 percent on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  * The peso languished below the 40.00 per dollar level, but traders said its falls would be limited by banks' dollar sales. \par \par \uc2  * Elsewhere, the ringgit was stronger despite Kuala Lumpur's apparent rejection of an IMF call for higher Malaysian interest rates in its recent annual review of the economy. \par \par \uc2  * Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad on Wednesday defended Malaysia's stance of keeping interest rates at current levels, saying businesses were already suffering. \par \par \uc2  * Bank Negara Malaysia accepted bids for one-month money at an average 11.04 percent and three-month at 11.07 percent under a new competitive bidding system, aimed at increasing transparency. \par \par \uc2  * Some dealers attributed the ringgit's rise to rumours Singapore had agreed to buy land owned by Malaysia's state railway firm on the island for 30 billion ringgit. \par \par \uc2  But Singapore Prime Minister Goh Chok Tong's office denied the rumours, \par \par \uc2  * In north Asia, the Hong Kong dollar was firm and forwards remained firm amid tight interbank liquidity and talk of overnight \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA). \par \par \uc2  * The HKMA said the outflow from the banking system on Wednesday was due to a commercial order and not related to speculative activities. \par \par \uc2  * The Taiwan dollar was steady while the Singapore dollar was slightly firmer. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4u06azn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
