\par\par \b \uc2  HK Dlr Weaker Late Despite Easing Of Local Interbank Rates \b0\par\par\uc2 352 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 1998\par 12:09\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong dollar was volatile early, but is hovering at weaker levels in a quiet \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market late Thursday, despite an easing of local interbank rates. \par \par \uc2  At 0939 GMT (5:39 a.m. EDT), the Hong Kong dollar is quoted at HK$7.7470 per U.S. dollar, weaker than late Wednesday's HK$7.7445. \par \par \uc2  In overseas trading late Wednesday, the Hong Kong dollar strengthened to HK$7.7445 per U.S. dollar on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and opened close to that level in the home market Thursday. It weakened later in the morning as a large U.S. bank and a mainland Chinese bank both bought U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  The suspected U.S. dollar buying by the HKMA in London was believed to be a move to counter a nearly US$1.0 billion withdrawal of U.S. dollars from the banking system by China Telecom (Hong Kong) Ltd. The HKMA also responded to the withdrawal, which left a glut of Hong Kong dollars in the home money market, by draining a nearly equivalent amount through its overnight discount window. That pushed the overnight interbank rate up to 6.5%. \par \par \uc2  But rates have eased Thursday on HKMA liquidity injections, said a dealer with a European bank. The overnight interbank rate is quoted at 4.50% late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong dollar forward premiums, after climbing on frenzied hedging in London and New York, have fallen back close to late Wednesday's levels in Hong Kong. \par \par \uc2  The one-month premium is at 35 points to the spot rate, up from 30 points late Wednesday but down from 50 points earlier Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The three-month premium is unchanged at 180 points from late Wednesday and down from 200 points Thursday morning. The six-month premium is also steady at 600 points, compared with late Wednesday's 610 points and early Thursday's 630 points. \par \par \uc2  -By Daisy S.Y. Wong; 852-2802-7002; dwong@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 30-04-98 \par \par \uc2  1009GMT \par \par \uc2  1053 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du4u03nb0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
