\par\par \b \uc2  Hong Kong Stocks Close 0.8% Lower; Market Sentiment Remains Grim \b0\par\par\uc2 613 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 1998\par 13:12\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG -(Dow Jones)- Hong Kong shares closed moderately lower Thursday after recouping sharper losses posted early in the session, but sentiment remains grim and further losses are expected in the week ahead, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Investors scooped up shares at the lower prices, which helped trigger the turnaround, but trading volume was thin and dominated by futures-related trade, they said. \par \par \uc2  The blue-chip Hang Seng Index lost 87.47 points, or 0.8%, to close at 10383.68, extending Wednesday's 207-point decline. The broader All Ordinaries Index finished down 44.13 points at 4808.24. Trading volume was worth HK$5.51 billion, down sharply from HK$8.17 billion in the previous session. \par \par \uc2  Futures contracts continue to trade at discounts to the cash market, indicating investors remain pessimistic about the market's near-term future, said Michael Ng, dealing director at Sassoon Securities. The May contract ended at 10310, a 73.68 discount to the cash market, while the June contract closed at 10370, a 13.68 point discount to the market. \par \par \uc2  Shares of market giant HSBC Holdings PLC helped temper the index's losses, bucking the market trend to end the day at HK$221.00, up HK$2.00. But analysts offered little reason for the bank's strength compared to other shares. \par \par \uc2  "This is where it should be. Fair value is (around) HK$220 in my opinion," said Steven Thompson, senior analyst at Nikko Research Centre (Hong Kong) Ltd., predicting HSBC will hug its current level for the moment. \par \par \uc2  Buying of HSBC was also driven by the futures market, said Annio Kwong, head of research at Asia Financial (Securities) Ltd. The share closed at HK$224.88 on the futures exchange. \par \par \uc2  The financial sub-index was the only one to post gains, rising 41.86 points to 15335.69. \par \par \uc2  Red chips were the hardest hit, with the sub-index losing 2% to 1457.09. Investors have been disappointed by recent earnings news from China-related companies and are also concerned about the slowdown in economic growth in China. \par \par \uc2  Short-term interest rates recovered Thursday, which also helped to ease concerns about the market and may have helped curtail losses, analysts said. On Wednesday, the overnight Hong Kong Interbank Offered Rate shot up to 6.5% from 4.25% a day earlier, following a huge sale of \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollars and the subsequent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The HKMA also went to great lengths to deny there was a speculative attack on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  underway. But by late Thursday, the overnight rate was back to 4.5%. \par \par \uc2  "The HKMA's denial that there was an attack (on the Hong Kong dollar) helped restore confidence," said Kwong. \par \par \uc2  But investors are still expected to remain cautious in the near term, analysts said. They attributed the market's recovery from sharper losses Thursday to investors realizing it had been a bit oversold in recent sessions. The benchmark index has slipped 9.9% since the beginning of the month. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Hong Kong market is taking its cues from the U.S. and Japan, rising and falling in tandem with the two big markets. The Nikkei's strong performance Thursday helped lift the Hong Kong market in the afternoon, Kwong said. But the market will be watching the Dow Jones Industrial Average for Friday's direction. The Nikkei 225 gained 1.6%, or 245.83 points, to 15641.26, erasing most of Tuesday's loss. \par \par \uc2  -By Kirsti Hastings; 852 2802-7002; Khastings@AP.org \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du4u00qpa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
