\par\par \b \uc2  HK stocks end sharply off in shadow of Japan, U.S. \b0\par\par\uc2 581 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, April 27 (Reuters) - Hong Kong stocks slumped to a sharply weaker close on Monday as soft overseas markets eroded investors' already low confidence, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  The Hang Seng Index shed 286.22 points, or 2.63 percent, to end at 10,593.71 after hitting a low of 10,555.57. \par \par \uc2  Volume picked up to HK$6.22 billion compared with Friday's quiet HK$4.27 billion. \par \par \uc2  The gloomy mood was expected to prevail but some brokers said the blue chip index may be approaching more stable levels with support at 10,500. \par \par \uc2  "There can always be a little bit of a bounce, but it is not going to be on impressive volume," said Glenn Lesko, head of sales at ABN-Amro Hoare Govett Asia Securities. \par \par \uc2  "I think we are in for a very slow quarter with a downward bias. That's the way it's been so far and I don't see any major reasons for it to change." \par \par \uc2  Local sentiment took a beating from markets in Tokyo where the yen lost ground and the key 225-share Nikkei average ended down 2.26 percent after a government stimulus package for the economy failed to impress investors. \par \par \uc2  Losses on Wall Street and in London on Friday further hampered trade, brokers said. \par \par \uc2  Steven Thompson, chief analyst at Nikko Research Center (HK), said the weakness of Japanese markets had little direct impact on the Hong Kong market. \par \par \uc2  "It should have a slightly positive effect," Thompson said. \par \par \uc2  "The weakness in Tokyo will result in some cash coming back into fund managers' hands." \par \par \uc2  Thompson said there may be some concerns about the Hong Kong dollar which has been hovering near the psychologically important level of HK$7.75 to the U.S. dollar for more than a week. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA) may have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped below HK$7.75, he said. \par \par \uc2  Futures put pressure on the market as they continued trading at a discount. The April Hang Seng Index contract finished down 420 points at 10,430 ahead of its expiry on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Index heavyweight HSBC Holdings  led the market lower, falling HK$5.00 to HK$222.00. Hang Seng Bank  lost HK$2.50 to HK$65.75. \par \par \uc2  Property counters were the biggest losers and the sub-index shaved off 2.97 percent. Sun Hung Kai Properties  was down HK$1.80 at HK$47.40 and Cheung Kong  dropped HK$0.75 to HK$51.75. \par \par \uc2  Henderson Land  closed HK$0.70 lower at HK$34.50. \par \par \uc2  The company said it had sold more than 1,000 of about 1,790 flats on sale at its Dawning Views development in the New Territories over the weekend. \par \par \uc2  The flats were offered at an average price of between HK$3,068 and about HK$4,100 per square foot. Planned apartment sales for the rest of 1998 could generate revenue of about HK$40 billion, the company said. \par \par \uc2  China Everbright  was down HK$0.35 at HK$5.50 ahead of its 1997 results, due later on Monday. The consensus forecast, according to Edinburgh Financial Publishing's The Estimate Directory, is for net profit of HK$264.5 million compared with a net loss of HK$202.27 million the previous year. \par \par \uc2  - Kristina Zetterlund (852) 2843 6358; Fax (852) 2845 0636 \par \par \uc2  - Email: hongkong.newsroom@reuters.com \par \par \uc2  *. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4r06s12\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
