\par\par \b \uc2  HKMA Sold/U.S. Dollars/Dealers -2: Hallmarks Of  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 178 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 April 1998\par 11:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 HONG KONG\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--Standing U.S. dollar sell orders by the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday kept the Hong Kong dollar from breaching a key support level, dealers said. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market activity Thursday afternoon saw the Hong Kong dollar weaken slightly but steadily to HK$7.7500 from HK$7.7495 earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  That level is typically a trigger point for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the HKMA, and Thursday was no exception. \par \par \uc2  Two dealers said they saw small U.S. dollar sell orders being executed by a large bank, with one noting that the timing and amount bore the hallmarks of an HKMA standing order aimed at cooling the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The transactions - believed to be for only several million Hong Kong dollars each - sent a strong signal to the market and allowed the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen back to HK$7.7496 at 0934 GMT (5:34 a.m. EDT), a dealer at a European bank said. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 23-04-98 \par \par \uc2  0947GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du4n03e01\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
