\par\par \b \uc2  HK Dollar Strengthens After Morning Scare Over Weak Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 304 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 1998\par 09:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--The Hong Kong dollar is strengthening late Friday after nervous sentiment pushed the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a key support level in morning trading. \par \par \uc2  At 0720 GMT (2:20 a.m. EST), traders said the Hong Kong dollar was hovering between $7.7492 to $7.7493 against the U.S. dollar , stronger than $7.7496 late Thursday and $7.7499 Friday morning. \par \par \uc2  A trader at a local bank credited a transaction made at HK$7.7470 against the U.S. dollar in the early afternoon as the reason for the Hong Kong dollar's turnaround from weak morning levels. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that although the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was close to HK$7.75, a level which typically triggers \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they detected no strong selling pressure to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  any weaker. \par \par \uc2  While local factors, including a subdued domestic economy and a couple of long weekends were cited as factors for the local dollar's slide in early trading, dealers said in the afternoon that they now believe it's Japan's market troubles that are overhanging the Hong Kong market. \par \par \uc2  'It is basically a 'Japan problem'. Hong Kong markets just reacted to it,' said a trader at a local bank. \par \par \uc2  He noted once news that Moodys' Investors Service downgraded Japan's outlook to negative from stable and the yen broke through 134 in the morning , forward premiums rate in Hong Kong shot up almost immediately. \par \par \uc2  'Banks were worried about the spillover effect from a weak Japanese yen and so they started borrowing long-term funds to protect themselves against possible rise in interest rates,' deputy chief dealer Wong Chi-yiu at Bank of China group treasury centre said. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) Dow Jones Newswires 03-04-98 \par \par \uc2  0736GMT \par \par \uc2  1053 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du4302tr0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
