\par\par \b \uc2  US/HK forwards end sharply firmer on yen woes. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Angi Li \par \uc2 576 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, April 3 (Reuters) - US/HK forwards ended sharply firmer on Friday as the fall of the Japanese yen below 134 per U.S. dollar lent a bid tone to the forward rates, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The local money market was becoming uneasy as the Japanese yen broke its key support at 134 per U.S. dollar, but market participants were less panicky than they were last October," a senior dealer at one of the note-issuing banks said. \par \par \uc2  Stephen Cheung, a director at Jardine Fleming Bank, said: "Market sentiment was still better and the market was more stable than in January or last October." \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar spot managed to defend the key support of HK$7.75 per U.S. dollar partly on firmer rates as well as rumours of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Hong Kong Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  (HKMA). \par \par \uc2  There was no major selling of the Hong Kong dollar on Friday, Frederic Laine, head of interest rates and derivatives trading at Credit Lyonnais, said. \par \par \uc2  The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  ended slightly stronger at HK$7.7473/83 per U.S. dollar after slipping to a three-month low of HK$7.7498/01, a hair away from the key support of HK$7.75, a level that traders said would trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the HKMA. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  opened at HK$7.7493/03 against HK$7.7490/00 at Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said at least one note-issuing bank was spotted buying Hong Kong dollar spot against the U.S. dollar and was believed to be acting on behalf of the HKMA in a bid to cap the strength of the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  But forward rates rose more sharply as dealers took short positions against the Hong Kong dollar. \par \par \uc2  The three-month dollar forward posted strong gains at 360-400 points against 270-300 in early trade and 230-260 at Thursday's close. \par \par \uc2  The six-month was up at 950-1,000 versus 880-930 points in early trade and 775-830 at the close. \par \par \uc2  "The bid tone on forwards was much stronger today than over the past few weeks," a senior dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers noted that buying orders were seen mainly from Singapore and London. \par \par \uc2  Local interbank rates rose partly on routine demand ahead of the long weekend. \par \par \uc2  The overnight rate rose to 5.6-5.87 percent versus 4.43-4.93 in early trade and 4.68-4.75 at Thursday's close. This compared with seven percent on January 6 and 300 percent on October 23 when the Hong Kong dollar came under speculative attack. \par \par \uc2  The one-month rose most sharply, by over 130-150 basis points to 7.06-7.18 versus 5.87-6.0 in early trade and 5.46-5.60 at the close. \par \par \uc2  The three-month posted a smaller 80 basis point jump, ending at 7.56-7.68 versus 7.18-7.37 and 6.75-7.00 at the close. \par \par \uc2  The six-month was firmer at 8.37-8.5 versus 7.75-8.12 at the open and 7.50-7.75 at the close. \par \par \uc2  Higher short-term rates encouraged some selling of the U.S. dollar for the Hong Kong dollar, strengthening the tone of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Jardine Fleming's Cheung said. \par \par \uc2  - Hong Kong Newsroom (852) 2847-4039; Fax 852-2845-0636. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du43084jl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
